<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Poundmaker</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1842–<a href="/years/1886">1886</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Pītikwahanapiwīyin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Poundmaker was a Plains <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cree">Cree</a> chief who accepted Treaty 6 with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> in 1876 and settled on a reserve near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battleford">Battleford</a>. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-west-rebellion">North-West Rebellion</a> of 1885 his band's warriors repulsed a Canadian attack at Cut Knife Hill, but he restrained them from pursuing the retreating troops. He surrendered, was imprisoned for treason and died soon after his release.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/poundmaker.jpg" alt="Poundmaker" width="320" height="474" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Prof. Buell, O.B. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Poundmaker.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Poundmaker was born in Rupert's Land near present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battleford">Battleford</a>, the child of Sikakwayan, an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assiniboine">Assiniboine</a> medicine man, and a mixed-blood <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cree">Cree</a> woman who was the sister of Chief Mistawasis. Orphaned young, he, his brother Yellow Mud Blanket and their younger sister were raised by their mother's Cree community under Chief Wuttunee, later the Red Pheasant Band. According to Cree oral tradition his name came from his gift, granted by spirit helpers, for drawing buffalo into pounds by singing and drumming. In 1873 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crowfoot">Crowfoot</a>, chief of the Blackfoot, adopted him, which increased his influence and strengthened ties between the two peoples.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Poundmaker came to prominence in the negotiations of Treaty 6 and formed his own band, which in 1881 settled on a reserve about 40 km northwest of Fort Battleford. He did not oppose the treaty but criticised the government's failure to keep its promises. In 1885, starving after the disappearance of the bison, his people travelled to Battleford, where oral accounts say he meant to reaffirm his loyalty to the Queen; the frightened townspeople had shut themselves in the police fort, and the Indian agent kept him waiting for two days. Who looted the abandoned town is disputed.</p><p>On 2 May 1885 Lieutenant-Colonel William Dillon Otter attacked the camp at Cut Knife Hill with 332 troops. After six hours of inconclusive fighting Otter withdrew, and Poundmaker, who had taken no part in the battle, persuaded the warriors not to pursue. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-riel">Louis Riel</a>'s defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batoche">Batoche</a> he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battleford">Battleford</a> to make peace with Major-General Middleton and surrendered on 26 May.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Convicted of treason on the basis of a letter written by Riel bearing his name, he was sentenced to three years in Stony Mountain Penitentiary. At his trial he is reported to have said that he had given himself up because he wanted justice. Through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crowfoot">Crowfoot</a>'s influence his hair was not cut and he served only seven months, but prison ruined his health, and he died of a lung haemorrhage on 4 July 1886. He was buried at Blackfoot Crossing and reburied on the Poundmaker Reserve in 1967. On 23 May 2019 Prime Minister Justin Trudeau exonerated him at Cut Knife Hill, since renamed Poundmaker Hill.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Poundmaker” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Some Cree under Big Bear and <b>Poundmaker</b> were drawn into the North-West Rebellion of 1885, after which the alliance broke up under reserve rule.</blockquote><a href="/state/iron-confederacy">Iron Confederacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Métis fighters defeated the North-West Mounted Police at Duck Lake on 26 March, while Cree warriors from Big Bear's and <b>Poundmaker's</b> bands fought separately at Frog Lake, Battleford and Cut Knife.</blockquote><a href="/state/provisional-government-of-saskatchewan">Provisional Government of Saskatchewan</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battleford">Battleford</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/canada">Canada</a>. Died in Gleichen, in what is now <a href="/countries/canada">Canada</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.74,-108.32&amp;year=1870" aria-label="Battleford on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Poundmaker"><image href="/maps/globes/g240n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5601 5566h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5601 5566h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.74,-108.32&amp;year=1870">What was here in 1870? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
